      title: 'New People, New Places, New Things',
      bible: { book: 'Proverbs', chapter: 13, verse_range: '20',
        key_verse: '"One who walks with wise men grows wise, but a companion of fools suffers harm." — Proverbs 13:20 (WEB)',
        devotion: 'Recovery often requires changing your environment. The friends who use, the bars you frequented, the routines that revolved around substances — these are not neutral. They are gravitational fields pulling you back. This is not judgment on those people — it is protection for you. You need to be ruthlessly honest about which environments support your recovery and which sabotage it.',
        reflection_q: 'Who in your life supports your recovery? Who makes it harder? What environments are you willing to leave behind to stay free?',
      },
      journal: { prompt: 'Make three lists: (1) People who support my recovery (2) People/places/situations I need to avoid (3) New people/places/activities I want to build into my life. Be honest, even if it is painful.', entry_type: 'general' },
      workout: { premade_id: 'strength-circuit', motivational_tip: 'The gym is one of the best "new places" in recovery. Surround yourself with people pursuing health.', coach_note: 'Consider finding a workout partner. Accountability in fitness translates to accountability in recovery.' },
      nutrition: { focus: 'Social eating', meal_theme: 'Recovery-friendly social meals', plan: 'Practice eating a meal with someone who supports your recovery. Social meals without substances can feel unfamiliar at first. Practice it. Order sparkling water with lime. It gets easier.', tip: 'Have a non-alcoholic drink in your hand at social events — it reduces questions and pressure.', recipe_search: 'dinner party non alcoholic celebration' },
      affirmation: 'I am building a new environment that supports my freedom. I choose wise companions who help me grow.',
