      title: 'Forgiving Yourself',
      bible: { book: '1 John', chapter: 1, verse_range: '9',
        key_verse: '"If we confess our sins, he is faithful and righteous to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness." — 1 John 1:9 (WEB)',
        devotion: 'You may have accepted that God forgives you. But have you forgiven yourself? Many people in recovery carry a debt they refuse to cancel — the debt against themselves. "I should have known better." "I wasted so many years." "I hurt my family." These are true statements. But if God — who is perfectly just — has forgiven you, who are you to override His judgment? Self-forgiveness is not excusing what happened. It is accepting the pardon that has already been given.',
        reflection_q: 'What have you not forgiven yourself for? What would it take to accept God\'s forgiveness as final?',
      },
      journal: { prompt: 'Write a forgiveness letter to yourself. Address the version of you who was in addiction. Acknowledge what happened with honesty. Then write: "I forgive you. You did not know what you know now. And from this point forward, we walk in freedom."', entry_type: 'spiritual' },
      workout: { premade_id: 'yoga-flow', motivational_tip: 'Release. Let go. Soften. Forgiveness is a physical act as much as a spiritual one.', coach_note: 'Breathe into every stretch. Release what you have been holding.' },
